{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a Hebrew slave.</b> Rashi explained: [this refers to] one sold by the court on account of his theft, as it is written ״ואם אין לו ונמכר בגניבתו״. Or perhaps it refers only to one who sold himself due to poverty — but one sold by the court does not go out after six. And there is a difficulty: how can one say that one sold by the court does not go out after six, when it is written in the section of ראה: ״כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה ועבדך שש שנים״ — ״כי ימכר״ suggests [sold] by others, and Rashi too explained it as a court sale. Therefore one may say that Rashi's explanation is a midrash according to the Mekhilta: ״כי תקנה״ implies that the son or the brother would serve [the master] — that if the master dies within the six [years], the son would serve in his place, and if he has no son, the brother who inherits would serve in his place. And regarding ״מכרוהו ב\"ד״ — do you say this about one sold by the court, or perhaps it refers only to one who sold himself due to poverty? About this we say: the son or the brother would serve; but one sold by the court does not serve the son and does not serve the brother, as it is written ״כי ימכר לך״ etc., which implies a court sale, and it is written ״ועבדך שש שנים״, which implies a restriction: ״ועבדך״ and not the heir. When it says ״כי ימוך אחיך ונמכר לך״ — this is one who sold himself, and there it is written ״עד שנת היובל יעבוד עמך״, which implies that he serves the son and the brother. How then do I fulfill ״כי תקנה״ [as referring to] a court sale? For if only this verse were written, I would have said that one sold by the court serves the son and the brother, since ״יעבוד״ implies that as well. Therefore it is also written regarding a court sale: ״כי ימכר לך אחיך״ etc., and it is written ״ועבדך שש שנים״, which implies: and not the heir. Thus you have an inclusion and a restriction regarding a court sale: we apply the inclusion to the son and the restriction to the brother, and we say regarding one sold by the court: he serves the son but not the brother. And in tractate Kiddushin they ask: what did you see to include the son and exclude the brother? And they answer: I include the son, for he stands in place of his father regarding היוודאה and regarding the field of inheritance, as is stated there. And furthermore, there is a difficulty for Rashi's explanation, for he explained that the novelty is in going out after six — if so, what does ״כי ימוך אחיך ונמכר לך״ come to say? This is one who sold himself — so what of it? Nevertheless this verse is needed for one who sold himself, to [teach] that he goes out after six, for from the verse ״כי ימוך״ we would hear only that he goes out at the Jubilee, as it is written ״עד שנת היובל יעבוד עמך״. And one may say: perhaps one who sold himself does not go out after six, and it is derived by way of a gezeirah shavah of ״שכיר — שכיר״ according to the one who holds [this derivation], for regarding one who sold himself it is written ״כשכיר כתושב״, and regarding one sold by the court it is written ״כי משנה שכר שכיר״."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:44.686562076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}